WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.047
(audience cheering)
- What's up, everybody?

00:00:01.047 --> 00:00:03.381
We're back with Lauren Graham and Mo Amer,

00:00:03.381 --> 00:00:05.430
give it up. (clapping)
(audience applauding)

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:08.597
So that was the look at
Mo's Netflix show, "MO."

00:00:08.597 --> 00:00:11.490
Do you really carry olive oil with you?

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:14.067
- I used to regularly
carry olive oil with me

00:00:14.067 --> 00:00:15.930
and I just ruined too many clothes.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:16.763
Like, you just.

00:00:16.763 --> 00:00:17.596
- Oh, exactly.
(Lauren laughs)

00:00:17.596 --> 00:00:18.990
- The madness had to stop.

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:19.823
I had to just like submit and just use-

00:00:19.823 --> 00:00:22.110
- [Kelly] I love olive oil.
- Yeah, it's a big thing

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:23.237
for us for sure.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:23.237 --> 00:00:26.220
- You could wear it, you
could use it for your skin,

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.330
use it for your hair.
- (laughs) Multipurpose.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:28.163
- You could drink it.

00:00:28.163 --> 00:00:29.046
Yeah, no, it's a whole
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:29.046 --> 00:00:29.879
- 'nother situation.

00:00:29.879 --> 00:00:30.870
- They always do that for vocals

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:32.370
when we were younger they'd
be like, take some olive oil.

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:34.140
I was like, "Is this fine?"
- Really?

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:35.220
- And then, yeah.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.480
I don't feel like it helped,

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:37.530
but they, whatever.
(Mo and audience laughing)

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.000
They'd give you whatever if you're sick.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.440
And I'd be like, "Alright."

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.790
So where exactly did you grow up?

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:44.010
'Cause I knew you were
in Texas for a lot of

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.170
your young life, right?
- Yeah, the whole series

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:49.500
was filmed in Houston,
the vast majority of it.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.540
We filmed it out of a suburb called Alief,

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.100
which has over 90 languages

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:54.347
spoken in Alief alone.
- That's cool.

00:00:54.347 --> 00:00:56.640
- One of the most diverse
neighborhoods in America.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:57.750
And I just thought it was absurd,

00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.430
there's never been a series,

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:01.500
narrative sitcom filmed out of Houston.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.880
Fourth largest city, huge
exporter of great music.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.680
- Yeah.
- And just never has had

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.810
its flowers, in my opinion.
- [Kelly] Great rodeos.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:12.270
- Yeah. Great rodeo, yeah.
- (laughs) I've been there.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:13.680
So how did you support yourself

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.780
while working on your comedy in Houston?

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.190
- When I was, I started stand-up

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:19.080
when I was 14 years old.
- [Lauren] Wow.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:20.610
- And-
- That's brave.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:21.690
- [Lauren] Yeah.
- Yeah, I came over as

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:24.510
a refugee immigrant to
America when I was nine.

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.760
So I didn't necessarily have
like a social security number,

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.580
couldn't get a job,
started working very young,

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.650
and became an entrepreneur,
you could say. (chuckles)

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.890
At like 14, I would sell
fake, I'll just spit it out,

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:37.113
I sold fake Versaces!
(Lauren and audience laughing)

00:01:37.113 --> 00:01:39.270
- Ver-sak-ee.
- Fake goods. Yeah. (laughs)

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:40.240
Ver-say-see, I sold the hell out of it.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.036
- Yeah, for some Goo-key. (laughs)

00:01:42.036 --> 00:01:45.540
- I would put on like a
dope pair of sunglasses

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.280
and you know, somebody would see them,

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:48.660
be like, "Hey man, those are really fly."

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:50.190
I was like, "Yeah, it's my last pair,"

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:51.510
and you could have 'em.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.190
And I'd give 'em a price
and they would buy 'em.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:54.360
Once they'd leave, I'd put on

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.670
another pair of sunglasses.
(Lauren and audience laughing)

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:57.900
It's just that. That's how it worked.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:58.733
- Well you fit right in,

00:01:58.733 --> 00:01:59.807
in Texas. (laughs)
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:59.807 --> 00:02:01.113
- With the salesmen, that's awesome.

00:02:01.113 --> 00:02:02.850
Have you ever bought anything knockoff?

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:04.000
Have you ever done...

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.300
- Yeah. Oh, a 1000%.
- [Mo] Yeah.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:07.260
- But that's a really good

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:08.654
sales tactic. (laughs)
(Mo and audience laughs)

00:02:08.654 --> 00:02:11.190
The old, the only one left.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:12.260
- [Mo] It's the craziest-
- It's on my face.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:13.350
- At the end of the week,

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:15.090
they'll just all be
wearing the same stuff.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:16.980
They all know each other
from the same neighborhood.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:18.840
So they'll come back
at me with some anger,

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.160
like, "You told me it was the last one."

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:21.630
I was like, "At that
moment, it was the last one.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:23.930
But I do have another one of a kind here."

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:24.780
(Lauren and Kelly laugh)
(audience laughs)

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:26.280
- I got a shipment in. (laughs)

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.284
- I swear. I would sell it, it would sell.

00:02:28.284 --> 00:02:29.475
- [Kelly] (laughs) Oh my God.

00:02:29.475 --> 00:02:30.434
- I'm not even kidding.

00:02:30.434 --> 00:02:31.590
- So your show, you said it's

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:32.970
based on your real life.
- Yeah.

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:35.640
- So go back and, is it hard to go back

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.230
and relive some of those
things in the episodes?

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:38.063
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:02:38.063 --> 00:02:39.900
I mean, some of them are
really difficult memories

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:44.040
of you know, fleeing war,
to coming to Houston.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:48.150
I recreated the home that
we fled from in Kuwait.

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.580
That was really special and unique,

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.410
and my mom would just cry immediately

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:55.200
when she watched that flashback.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.510
And yeah, it was just
really, really special

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:01.530
and to you know, bring back your father,

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:02.940
essentially bringing him back to life

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:04.820
and recreating really painful memories,

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:06.900
at the same time is very cathartic

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.605
to just put that out there.

00:03:08.605 --> 00:03:11.280
In some cases I realized
I didn't even deal

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.050
with those things in my regular life.

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:15.176
And as we were filming
I was like, "Ah, man."

00:03:15.176 --> 00:03:16.009
- [Kelly] It's like therapeutic.

00:03:16.009 --> 00:03:17.414
- It's really therapeutic.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:17.414 --> 00:03:19.333
- It was really rewarding to do that.

00:03:19.333 --> 00:03:21.330
- A beautiful way to honor him as well.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:23.430
That's cool. Yeah.
- Absolutely.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.100
- So you included a
special tribute to your mom

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:27.420
in one of the episodes, right?

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:28.253
- Absolutely.
- Yeah?

00:03:28.253 --> 00:03:30.210
What was that like?
- So you know, the dedicated,

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.070
the episode two is called Yamo,

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:36.540
which is a Syrian slang term for mother.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:38.730
And there was a song that was

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:42.900
on a classic Syrian TV show
called (speaks arabic),

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.240
which is named after its main character.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.910
I've seen my father cry
on two separate occasions,

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:50.310
was when my grandmother passed away

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.080
and when he was watching this

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.180
particular scene from (speaks arabic),

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.130
and they were singing a song called Yamo.

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:59.820
And the entire song is
essentially a prayer for mothers.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:02.610
It's, you know, nine
months you carried me.

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:04.290
How many times did I make you tired

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:05.520
while you were raising me?

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.170
How many pairs of socks
did you dry out for me?

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:10.500
How many times did you
go hungry to feed me?

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.120
It is just really, really special.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:15.600
And the end of it is
like an entire prayer is,

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.020
you know only God is enough for you.

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:21.750
And may God give you a
long life and preserve you.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:23.280
It was just so sweet.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.200
And that song was super hard to get

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.810
because how do you get
a song that came out

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:30.000
on a classic TV show in the Middle East?

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.080
There's no like-

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.960
- Copyright? No, yeah.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:35.340
- There's no roadmap to that.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:38.130
So our great music
supervisor, Suhel Nafar,

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:39.750
got in touch with (speaks arabic),

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:42.900
the creator of the
show, in war-torn Syria.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:45.780
And I got a letter from him directly,

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:50.730
saying that I can use the song
for the show, 30 years later.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.130
And I put it over my mom making olive oil

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:54.900
in the series as a dedication to mothers.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.499
- Oh my gosh.
(audience applauding)

00:04:57.499 --> 00:04:59.940
I love how meaningful that is.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:01.470
Like, it's so thoughtful.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:02.850
- Yeah. You're gonna make me cry on

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:03.969
daytime television.
- [Kelly] I'm so sorry.

00:05:03.969 --> 00:05:05.430
- This is ridiculous.
- No, I just think it's

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:06.780
such a beautiful thing

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:08.640
and that's what I love about music,

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:09.690
I feel so blessed to be a part of it

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:11.490
because it's like they're memories.

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.350
Like music is almost like
memories, like smells.

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:14.940
You know, like the one you
won't tell us you're wearing.

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:15.773
- [Mo] Yes.
(Lauren and audience laughing)

00:05:15.773 --> 00:05:16.606
- They're nostalgic.

00:05:16.606 --> 00:05:18.510
Like, it's like a nostalgia that you get

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:20.760
with certain things in life,
and music is one of them.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:21.600
It's beautiful.
- [Mo] No absolutely-

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.340
- 30 years later it's
still impacting people.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:24.780
- Yeah, I saw that when I was like

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:26.760
five, six years old and to have it

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:28.230
in my own series,
- [Kelly] Cool.

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:29.760
- this many years later,

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:31.047
is just incredible, you know?
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:31.047 --> 00:05:32.100
- How can you write that?

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.350
Like, all the things that had to happen

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:36.120
for me to be where I am today.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:39.210
For my mom to flee
Palestine, end up in Kuwait.

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:42.630
To flee war in Kuwait to
end up in Houston, Texas.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:44.700
To go to the Houston
Livestock Show and Rodeo

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:47.377
when I was 10 to see live
comedy for the first time,

00:05:47.377 --> 00:05:48.420
"I'm gonna be a comedian."

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:51.030
Four years later, I'm doing
standup, and now I'm here.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:52.200
It's just crazy.
(audience applauding)

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:54.150
- That is crazy, it's awesome.

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:58.830
And do you think like, cause
I was a cocktail waitress

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:00.330
at a comedy club before all of this.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:01.463
- [Mo] Oh, were you really?
- I was, and-

00:06:01.463 --> 00:06:02.399
- Me too.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:02.399 --> 00:06:03.360
- [Mo] Were you really?
- I think

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:04.560
I knew this about you.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.610
And I love though, like, I
feel like a lot of comedians,

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:12.360
it's so funny because there
can be kind of a dark past,

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:13.410
something that happened that almost

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:15.750
made them find humor.
- Sure.

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:16.590
- Do you think, 'cause like,

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:19.410
I'm like picturing you one
to nine years old like,

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:21.900
in a war-ridden, like that
had to have been hard.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:22.733
- It was hard.
- Do you think that that has

00:06:22.733 --> 00:06:25.050
kind of fueled you like to find humor?

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:26.520
- Absolutely, it was
incredibly therapeutic,

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:28.200
and the way I got into it was,

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:30.630
in high school, my father passed away.

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:31.753
I started skipping class all the time.

00:06:31.753 --> 00:06:33.360
I was living Ferris Bueller's Day Off.

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:34.890
I would go to baseball games.
(Kelly and Lauren laugh)

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:38.070
I just didn't care, and my
teacher just realigned me.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:41.130
She said, "Don't you wanna
be a stand-up comedian?

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:43.620
You need to graduate and find a purpose.

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:44.580
I'll tell you what, I'll let you do

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:46.920
stand-up in class every Friday."

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:48.480
Mrs. Reed, shout out Mrs. Reed,

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:49.530
Mrs. Brodrick.
- That's cool.

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:50.670
- And I did standup.

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:52.560
She goes, "As long as you
can include Shakespeare

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:54.570
in your standup here and there, I will,

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:55.950
you know give you extra credit."

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:57.260
I was like, all about it.
- Challenge accepted.

00:06:57.260 --> 00:06:58.657
- I was like, "To be or not to be."

00:06:58.657 --> 00:07:00.120
(Kelly and audience laugh)
You know, it was like

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:01.197
immediately.

00:07:01.197 --> 00:07:04.170
And then she took me to theater
arts department weeks later.

00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:06.690
She's like, "This kid's
been doing original stand-up

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:08.040
and doing all these accents.

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:09.570
I think he belongs here."
- [Kelly] Wow.

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:11.490
- And then I started getting
lead roles in theater

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:13.770
and just became an honorary thespian.

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:15.210
And she changed my life.

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:16.380
Yeah, she changed my life.
- Isn't that,

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:17.760
I love teachers so much.
- [Mo] Yeah, yeah.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:18.593
- We talk about it all
(audience applauds)

00:07:18.593 --> 00:07:19.470
the time on the show.

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:21.813
My mama was a teacher. They're angels.

